WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.652
Project B changes the output grain from patient to department.

00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:06.602
The outer query groups admissions by department.

00:00:06.602 --> 00:00:11.002
It calculates total admissions, distinct patients, and average stay.

00:00:11.002 --> 00:00:15.442
A correlated subquery adds the count of open admissions for the current department.

00:00:15.442 --> 00:00:20.977
This shows that correlation can use a grouping value such as department, not only a patient ID.

00:00:20.977 --> 00:00:26.102
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:26.102 --> 00:00:30.962
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.962 --> 00:00:37.787
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:42.777
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:47.600
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
